<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hashid</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hashid are one of the two great tribal confederations of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakil">Bakil</a>, living in the highlands north of Sanaa. Long a main military support of the Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>, they are known for their cohesion, and their paramount sheikhs of the al-Ahmar family became leading figures in the Yemen Arab Republic and in unified Yemen after 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hashid.jpg" alt="Hashid" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ibrahem Qasim · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheikh_sadiq_al-ahmar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Hashid was already a well-known tribe in the 1st millennium BCE and appears often in Sabaic inscriptions. With the tribes Humlan and Yarsum it belonged to a confederation called the Sum'ay, which worshipped the god Ta'lab. The inscriptions name the Banu Hamdan as chiefs of Hashid; the Hamdan later gained control of part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakil">Bakil</a> and gave their clan name to a confederation that took in both. Medieval Yemeni genealogies make Hashid and Bakil the sons of Jashim bin Jubran, a descendant of Hamdan. In the late 3rd century the Hamdan tribes changed their allegiance to Himyar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Muhammad first sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> to call the Yemenis to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and when the Hamdani tribes of the highlands responded coldly he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, who converted them. After Muhammad's death the Hamdan stayed Muslim and kept out of the ridda movement. Late in the 9th century they invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> al-Hadi Yahya bin al-Hussain to Sa'da, and from then on Zaidi teaching prevailed in the north. Writers have often called Hashid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakil">Bakil</a> the two wings of the Zaidi imamate, though many tribes refused the imams the temporal and legal role they claimed, and imams down to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-muhammad-hamid-ed-din">Imam Yahya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a> complained of the tribes' political power.</p><p>After the 1962 revolution <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-ibn-husayn-al-ahmar">Abdullah ibn Husayn al-Ahmar</a> led the confederation for decades as its paramount shaykh. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a>, who relied on this coalition to take power in 1978, and served as Speaker of Parliament until his death on 29 December 2007. His son Sadiq al-Ahmar succeeded him. In 2011 the new leadership sided with the protesters of the Arab Spring and launched a Hashid insurgency, which played a central part in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a> and in the protests that forced Saleh to step down in February 2012.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The break between the al-Ahmar family and Saleh, who kept the loyalty of some Hashid tribes, divided the confederation. This division, together with the suspension of Saudi financial support over the alliance of the al-Ahmar family with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a>, contributed to the confederation's defeat in the conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a>, and many of its tribes, among them the Bani Suraim, Usaimat and Uzer, made peace with the rebels or joined them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hashid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most Yemenis lived in villages, farming terraced highlands and wadis, and society in the north was organised around tribes, above all the <b>Hashid</b> and Bakil confederations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Politics remained dominated by army officers and tribal leaders of the <b>Hashid</b> and Bakil confederations.</blockquote><a href="/state/yemen-arab-republic">Yemen Arab Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Imams depended on the support of tribes such as <b>Hashid</b> and Bakil. al-Mansur Abd Allah ibn Hamza in the 13th century revived its strength against the Ayyubids, and it competed with the Sulayhids, Rasulids and Tahirids.</blockquote><a href="/state/zaydi-imamate-of-yemen">Zaydi Imamate of Yemen</a></li></ul></section>
